# The association idiom (Ruling 1, fix/recursion-leak-and-fifth-amber-site;
# proven at PROBE_PARSER.md capability 7). Records are structural: a
# record's field set is part of its shape, and every field a program reads
# must be a name known when the source is written -- that is what lets
# shapes.py's origins_of trace a value back to where it came from. `r[k]`
# for a computed `k` would make fields dynamically addressable and break
# that guarantee, so Planes has no dynamic, string-keyed lookup into a
# record. A list of records plus a linear `for each ... where` scan gives
# the same capability -- every field access below is still a literal name.

to lookup of table, key:
  result = nothing
  for each entry in table where entry.name == key:
    result = entry.value
  give result

prices = [{ name: "widget", value: 12 },
          { name: "gadget", value: 30 },
          { name: "gizmo", value: 7 }]

hit = lookup of prices, "gadget"
miss = lookup of prices, "nonexistent"

show hit
show miss
